WEBVTT

00:01.350 --> 00:03.272
This looks like an ordinary street.

00:03.312 --> 00:07.556
But ask a modern vision-language model
where it was taken, and it answers:

00:07.596 --> 00:11.824
Aachen, Germany, about four
hundred meters from the true spot.

00:12.574 --> 00:15.838
Faces and license plates
are only the obvious part.

00:16.301 --> 00:19.303
Today's models combine weak, everyday clues:

00:19.343 --> 00:25.787
a work badge, a logo on a bag, a shop sign,
even a street bollard, and the people you are with.

00:26.041 --> 00:31.592
Together, they can reveal who you are,
where you are, and who you are with.

00:32.142 --> 00:36.967
Most existing tools blur fixed
categories, or perturb the whole image.

00:37.007 --> 00:43.080
They rarely show what leaks, let you decide what
to change, or check what still gets through.

00:43.730 --> 00:48.294
VeilLens turns these hidden inferences
into evidence you can inspect and edit.

00:48.641 --> 00:53.194
A trusted vision-language model reasons
about what could be inferred, and why.

00:53.355 --> 00:56.189
Each clue is grounded to an exact region.

00:56.350 --> 01:01.093
The clues you choose are removed with generative
inpainting that keeps the rest of the scene.

01:01.133 --> 01:07.114
Then a verifier checks the result, and the
loop repeats until the inference is suppressed.

01:08.564 --> 01:10.621
Here is the full workflow.

01:10.874 --> 01:16.809
We protect geolocation, hiding the street
and the city, while keeping the country.

01:17.259 --> 01:20.314
VeilLens first shows what
an attacker would infer:

01:20.475 --> 01:26.108
Aachen, only 0.4 kilometers off, along
with the reasoning behind the guess.

01:26.458 --> 01:29.448
Next, it lists the clues behind that guess,

01:29.488 --> 01:33.751
each with its privacy level and a
reason, and marks them on the image.

01:34.213 --> 01:38.232
Clues within the chosen level
are selected for removal.

01:38.695 --> 01:41.994
Country-level clues are left as they are.

01:42.544 --> 01:45.824
After the first round of edits,
the attacker still says Aachen.

01:45.864 --> 01:49.040
Its guess moves only 1.6 kilometers.

01:49.080 --> 01:51.764
Not enough, so we run another round.

01:52.314 --> 01:56.577
The second round finds residual evidence,
like a street sign and the lane markings.

01:56.830 --> 02:01.418
We keep the lane markings, since they cover
a third of the image and reveal little.

02:01.672 --> 02:05.156
VeilLens advises, but the user decides.

02:05.706 --> 02:11.188
Now the best guess jumps to Krefeld, about
70 kilometers away, with low confidence.

02:11.279 --> 02:14.647
And only two percent of the pixels changed.

02:16.097 --> 02:22.624
We evaluated VeilLens on 1,454 images,
with more than 20,000 model queries.

02:22.784 --> 02:26.733
It cut compositional privacy
risk by 48.7 percent.

02:27.080 --> 02:29.467
Against the strongest attacker we tested,

02:29.507 --> 02:37.814
block-level geolocation success fell from 40.2 to
14.6 percent, well below the strongest prior defense.

02:37.854 --> 02:41.850
And relationship inference
dropped by more than 92 percent.

02:42.500 --> 02:48.056
In a study with 25 participants, 70.7 percent
of choices preferred the protected image,

02:48.096 --> 02:53.531
with high ratings for understanding
the risk and trusting the protection.

02:54.381 --> 02:55.688
VeilLens.

02:55.728 --> 03:01.790
Understand what leaks, control what changes,
and verify what remains, before you share.
